      "narrative": "The rain taps a slow rhythm against the shuttered windows of the Copper Finch. Across the scarred table, Mara Venn's fingers rest around a clay cup, her sharp eyes fixed on you as you ask about the Ash Gate.\n\nShe tilts her head, a faint crease forming between her brows. \"The Ash Gate,\" she says, voice low and measured. \"It's been sealed for near a decade, since the Grey Wardens locked it and walked away. No one goes through. No one comes out.\" She pauses, studying you. \"Old Bram used to tend it \u2014 the ferryman. But he's dead now. Found in the shallows three winters past.\"\n\nShe waits, letting the weight of that settle. Then she adds, \"Why do you ask?\"\n\nThe answer she's given is clean and factual \u2014 but as you listen, you realize you've learned nothing useful. No *why* behind the sealing. No clue what's on the other side. Just names and dates that lead nowhere. You missed whatever thread she was offering, and now the conversation has stalled.\n\nMara's eyes narrow slightly. She noticed your distraction. \"Was there something else, or are you just collecting old stories over breakfast?\"\n\n---\n\n**What do you say?**..."
    },
