      "narrative": "The name leaves your lips and hangs in the damp air like smoke.\n\n*\"Old Bram!\"*\n\nThe sound carries. It skims across the wet grass, glances off the black stone of the arch, and fades into the grey nothing beyond. No echo. The Gate swallows it whole.\n\nThe cloaked figure at the base stirs. A slow, deliberate movement \u2014 the lift of a head from a slumped posture. The travel-stained cloak shifts, and you catch a glimpse of a sharp jaw, dark eyes blinking against the grey light.\n\nShe's been awake this whole time. Listening.\n\nHer voice comes out rough, as if she hasn't spoken in days. \"Old Bram's dead. Has been for three winters.\" She studies you from beneath the hood's shadow. \"You're not from around here, are you? Nobody who knew Bram calls his name at the Gate.\"\n\nShe doesn't rise. Doesn't reach for a weapon. Just watches you with the patient stillness of someone who's learned to wait.\n\nThe Gate looms behind her. No wind passes through its arch. No birds call from the clearing's edge.\n\nShe waits for your answer."
    },
