      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch, fingers brushing the wet grass beneath the arch. The soil is cold, packed firm \u2014 undisturbed by recent digging or scraping. You sweep your palm across the ground near the base of the left pillar, then the right. Nothing. No loose stones, no disturbed earth, no hidden catch or seam. Just rain-soaked grass and the same unremarkable dirt that surrounds the entire clearing.\n\nThe cloaked woman watches your search with a tilt of her head. A faint, humorless smile touches her lips.\n\n\"You're looking for something that isn't there.\" Her voice carries no mockery, only flat certainty. \"I've been sitting at this Gate for three days. I've traced every crack in that stone, every blade of grass within arm's reach. There's nothing hidden beneath this arch.\"\n\nShe shifts, the leather satchel creaking as she adjusts her weight. \"But you asked what's hidden *here*.\" She taps two fingers against the black stone beside her. \"The Gate itself. That's what's hidden. The mechanism that opens it was sealed from the inside \u2014 the lock chamber, the counterweights, the release. All of it behind a foot of worked stone and warded iron. Whatever opens this Gate, you won't find it by scratching at the dirt.\"\n\nShe cocks her head, dark eyes sharpening. \"What's your name, stranger? And why does a dead Gate matter to you?\"\n\nThe cold air settles around you. The Gate waits, silent and unyielding."
    },
